	shorttitle = {{SeisTutor}},
	url = {https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/14/7/4167},
	doi = {10.3390/su14074167},
	abstract = {Education is the cornerstone of improving people’s lives and achieving global sustainability. Intelligent systems assist sustainable education with various beneﬁts, including recommending a personalized learning environment to learners. The classroom learning environment facilitates human tutors to interact with every learner and obtain the opportunity to understand the learner’s psychology and then provide learning material (access learner previous knowledge and well-align the learning material as per learner requirement) to them accordingly. Implementing this cognitive intelligence in Intelligent Tutoring System is quite tricky. This research focused on mimicking human tutor cognitive intelligence in the computer-aided system of offering an exclusive curriculum or quality education for sustainable learners. The prime focus of this research article was to evaluate the proposed SeisTutor using Kirkpatrick four-phase evaluation model. The experimental results depict the enhanced learning gained through intelligence incorporated SeisTutor against the intelligence absence, as demonstrated.},
	language = {en},
	number = {7},
	urldate = {2026-03-28},
	journal = {Sustainability},
	author = {Singh, Ninni and Gunjan, Vinit Kumar and Mishra, Amit Kumar and Mishra, Ram Krishn and Nawaz, Nishad},
	month = mar,
	year = {2022},
	pages = {4167},
	annote = {High relevanceee
Paper Summary
Title: SeisTutor: A Custom-Tailored Intelligent Tutoring System and Sustainable Education Authors: Ninni Singh, Vinit Kumar Gunjan, Amit Kumar Mishra, Ram Krishn Mishra, Nishad Nawaz Year: 2022 Journal: Sustainability (MDPI) — Vol. 14, Issue 7

What the Paper Does
This paper presents SeisTutor, a fully implemented intelligent tutoring system (ITS) built for the domain of Seismic Data Interpretation (a petroleum engineering subject). SeisTutor personalizes the learning experience by assessing each learner's prior knowledge and learning style before the session begins, then recommending a custom-tailored curriculum. During the session, it also uses a CNN-based facial emotion recognition module to capture the learner's psychological state in real time via webcam. The system is evaluated using the Kirkpatrick four-phase evaluation model across 60 participants split into two study groups: one with full personalization (Study 1) and one without (Study 2).

What It Covers
1. System Architecture — Four Components
SeisTutor follows the standard ITS architecture:


Domain Model: Organizes learning content for Seismic Data Interpretation into concepts and learning units, with content-level adaptation based on grasping level and learning style.


Pedagogy Model: Contains a Curriculum Planner (generates personalized learning sequence), a Learning Assessment module (tracks quiz performance), and an Understanding Assessment module (measures comprehension via summary test + dictionary-based sentiment analysis).


Learner Model: Tracks three things — learner demographic info, psychological state (emotion), and performance. The emotion recognition module triggers as soon as a learning session begins, capturing a webcam snapshot every 30 seconds and classifying it into one of 7 emotions: Happy, Sad, Surprise, Fear, Angry, Neutral, and Disgust.


Learner Interface: Communicates content via audio, video, text, and navigation tools.


2. CNN-Based Emotion Recognition Module
The emotion module is described in a flow diagram (Figure 3). Every 30 seconds, a webcam snapshot is taken and passed through a CNN-based classifier that assigns one of the 7 emotion labels. These results are stored in the database and used later for Kirkpatrick Phase 1 evaluation (Evaluation of Reaction). Crucially, the emotion results are logged and analyzed after the session — they are not used in real time to adapt the content during the session.
3. Pretest System
Two pretests are administered before learning begins:


Prior Knowledge Test (20 questions): Classifies the learner into Beginner, Intermediate, or Expert, and determines the curriculum accordingly.


Learning Style Test (18 questions): Classifies the learner into one of four styles: Imagistic, Acoustic, Intuitive, or Active (I²A² model).


Together, these determine the personalized tutoring strategy from 12 possible strategies.
4. Evaluation — Kirkpatrick Four Phases


Phase 1 (Reaction): Emotion data from the CNN module. Among 28 Study 1 participants: 44\% happy, 40\% neutral, 36\% angry, 32\% surprise, 30\% fear, 24\% sad — suggesting overall satisfaction with the learning content.


Phase 2 (Learning): Study 1 participants showed 22\% mean learning gain vs. 12\% in Study 2. Pearson correlation between learning gain and degree of understanding was 0.484 (p {\textless} 0.01), confirming a statistically significant relationship.


Phase 3 (Behaviour): Learner feedback surveys showed 93\% overall satisfaction. 82\% agreed that psychological state was accurately detected.


Phase 4 (Results): Paired-sample T-test: Study 1 T-stat = 11.410 (p {\textless} 0.01), Study 2 T-stat = 5.312 (p {\textless} 0.01). Both studies showed pre-to-post improvement, but Study 1 showed significantly higher gains, validating the benefit of personalized, emotion-aware learning.



