Why It IS Relevant
FER component is present and implemented ✅ SeisTutor contains a real, deployed CNN-based facial emotion recognition module used in an educational system with real students. You can cite this as an example of FER being embedded within an ITS — even if the emotion data does not drive the adaptation directly.
Real students in a real educational experiment ✅ 60 participants from an actual university setting, with control and treatment groups, pretest/post-test design, and statistical analysis. This supports your thesis's claim that such systems can be evaluated rigorously in academic settings.
Adaptive learning system with demonstrated learning gain ✅ The 22\% vs. 12\% learning gain comparison directly supports your thesis's premise: personalized, affect-aware systems outperform standard ones. You can cite this as empirical justification for building an adaptive system.
Kirkpatrick evaluation model ✅ If your thesis uses or references the Kirkpatrick model for evaluation, SeisTutor is a direct example of it being applied to an emotion-aware ITS — and you can cite it for that purpose specifically.
Emotion labels partially overlap with your dataset ✅ SeisTutor classifies 7 basic emotions including Happy, Sad, Angry, Neutral, Fear, Surprise — partially overlapping with your Kaggle dataset's educational labels (Confused, Engaged, Frustrated, Bored, Drowsy, Looking Away). You can use this contrast to argue that general FER emotion taxonomies are insufficient for education-specific scenarios, justifying your choice of an education-specific dataset.

Why It is NOT Fully Relevant
Emotion does NOT drive adaptation ❌ This is the most serious issue. The CNN emotion module in SeisTutor is used only for logging and post-session analysis — not for real-time content adaptation. The curriculum is decided entirely by pretest scores. Your thesis is specifically about using detected facial emotion to adapt the learning experience. SeisTutor does NOT do this. If you cite it as an example of "emotion-driven adaptive learning," that would be inaccurate.
General emotion labels, not education-specific ❌ SeisTutor uses standard FER emotion categories (happy, sad, angry, etc.) rather than education-specific engagement states like confused, frustrated, or bored. This is a gap you can point to explicitly.
Niche domain — petroleum engineering ❌ Seismic data interpretation has very limited overlap with general student learning contexts. It is not generalizable to typical classroom or e-learning scenarios your thesis targets.
Lower journal quality ⚠️ Sustainability (MDPI) is indexed and peer-reviewed, but it is a broad multidisciplinary journal with a high acceptance rate. It is not a specialized AI or education technology journal like IEEE Transactions or Computers \& Education. Your supervisor may note this.

Important Note — The Core Research Gap This Creates for You
SeisTutor's critical limitation is that the emotion module is passive — it observes but does not respond. The paper itself does not claim otherwise. This is actually a valuable research gap for your thesis to cite:

Singh et al. (2022) integrated a CNN-based facial emotion recognition module into SeisTutor; however, the system does not use detected emotions to adapt content in real time — emotion data is collected for post-session analysis only. Maya's proposed system addresses this gap by using facial emotion detection to directly trigger adaptive learning responses during the session.

This makes SeisTutor a useful "before" example — a system that has FER but hasn't yet closed the loop between emotion detection and adaptation, which is exactly what your thesis aims to do.

How to Use This Paper in Your Thesis
Chapter 2 — Literature Review (ITS Systems subsection):

"Singh et al. (2022) developed SeisTutor, an ITS that incorporates a CNN-based facial emotion recognition module to track learner psychological state via webcam during learning sessions. While the system personalized curricula based on pretest performance, detected emotions were not used to modify content in real time — they were stored for post-session evaluation purposes. This reflects a common limitation in existing ITS designs: emotion detection is implemented but not yet integrated into the adaptive feedback loop."

Chapter 2 — Research Gaps:

"A recurring limitation across FER-integrated educational systems is the passive use of emotion data. Singh et al. (2022) and AL-anazi et al. (2025) both collect emotion signals during learning but do not use them to drive real-time content adaptation — a gap this thesis directly addresses."

Chapter 5 — Future Work:

"Following Singh et al. (2022), future iterations of this system could incorporate a multi-phase evaluation framework such as the Kirkpatrick model to assess not only classification accuracy but also learner reaction, learning gain, and behavioral impact."


Bottom Line
Do NOT keep this as a core citation. It is a weak match to your thesis because the FER component does not drive adaptation — which is your thesis's central contribution. However, it has selective use value in two specific places: as a contrast example to highlight the research gap your thesis fills (systems that detect emotion but don't act on it), and as a reference for the ITS architecture section if you describe the standard four-component ITS design. Use it sparingly and never present it as a direct parallel to your work.
