@article{woolf_face_2023,
	title = {{FACE} {READERS}: {The} {Frontier} of {Computer} {Vision} and {Math} {Learning}},
	shorttitle = {{FACE} {READERS}},
	url = {https://commons.clarku.edu/faculty_computer_sciences/6},
	journal = {24th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence in Education Workshop},
	author = {Woolf, Beverly and Betke, Margrit and Yu, Hao and Bargal, Sarah Adel and Arroyo, Ivan and Magee, John and Allessio, Danielle and Rebelsky, William},
	month = jul,
	year = {2023},
	annote = {Here is a detailed summary and relevance assessment:

Paper Summary
Title: FACE READERS: The Frontier of Computer Vision and Math Learning Authors: Woolf, Betke, Yu, Bargal, Arroyo, Magee, Allessio \& Rebelsky Year: 2023 Venue: AIED 2023 Workshop — "Towards the Future of AI-Augmented Human Tutoring in Math Learning," Tokyo, Japan Published via: CEUR Workshop Proceedings (open access) Institutions: University of Massachusetts Amherst, Boston University, Georgetown University, Clark University

What the Paper Does
This paper presents a research survey describing the FaceReaders system — a suite of computer vision tools that use facial expression recognition to identify, monitor, and predict student engagement, motivation, and problem-solving behaviour during online math tutoring. The system is integrated with MathSpring, a freely available intelligent math tutoring platform. The paper structures the work across three sequential research phases, from dataset collection through deep learning model training to outcome prediction.

What It Covers
1. Core Motivation Students interacting with online learning systems experience emotions — confusion, excitement, frustration, anxiety — that correlate strongly with learning gains. Making this affective information accessible to teachers or digital tutors can identify when and which students need help. Real-time teacher needs include: Who needs my help most right now? Who is giving up? Is the class ready to move on?
2. Phase I — Dataset Collection and Labelling
The team collected and publicly released two video datasets of real students solving math problems via webcam while using MathSpring:


Dataset 1: 1,596 video clips from 30 students


Dataset 2 (extended): 2,749 video clips from 54 students


Annotation subset: 18,721 frames from 400 video clips labelled for attention/engagement via crowdsourcing (MTurk)


Behaviour labels used (automatically extracted from MathSpring's log data):




Label


Meaning




SOF


Solved on first attempt, no hints




ATT


Solved after 1 incorrect attempt, no hints




SHINT


Solved after seeing one or more hints




GUESS


Solved after multiple incorrect attempts, no hints




GIVEUP


Attempted but did not solve




SKIP


Skipped with no action




NOTR


First action too rapid — likely did not read problem




Engagement labels (from crowdworkers): "looking at screen", "looking at paper", "wandering"
Features extracted per frame using OpenFace: 18 AU presence values, 17 AU intensity values, head pose and eye gaze vectors — aggregated into a 376-dimensional feature representation.
3. Phase II — Attention Detection
The team trained deep learning classifiers to detect student attention/engagement from video in real-time. CNN architectures pretrained on ImageNet were fine-tuned to classify students as "looking at screen", "looking at paper", or "wandering." Results showed that CNNs significantly outperformed head pose estimation (94\% vs 60\% accuracy). The system was integrated into MathSpring and piloted in Summer 2021, delivering real-time re-engagement interventions (animated character, focus circle, message) when wandering was detected.
4. Phase III — Predicting Problem-Solving Outcomes
The team built and evaluated deep learning models to predict which of the seven behaviour labels a student would exhibit — predicting whether they would solve on first attempt, ask for hints, give up, or skip — based solely on facial affect signals from video. Their transfer learning approach (ATL-BP: Affect Transfer Learning for Behavior Prediction) achieved a 50\% relative increase in mean F-score and an 11 percentage point absolute accuracy improvement over their baseline. The model uses transfer learning from general facial affect recognition to the specific domain of student behaviour prediction in webcam videos.
5. Adaptive Intervention Design The system uses predicted behaviour labels to trigger appropriate ITS interventions: example problems, formulas, hints, or easier problems when "give up" is predicted. Animated learning companions provide emotional support and encourage a growth mindset.

Relevance Assessment to Your Thesis




