	annote = {DONE
shklha review paper helwa jedan jedan
Here is a detailed summary and relevance assessment:

Paper Summary
Title: Emotions Matter: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of the Detection and Classification of Students' Emotions in STEM during Online Learning Authors: Anwar, Rehman, Nasralla, Khattak \& Khilji Year: 2023 Journal: Education Sciences (MDPI) — open access, peer-reviewed

What the Paper Does
This is a large, rigorous PRISMA-based systematic review and meta-analysis (39 pages) that examines how sentiment analysis — both text-based and facial — has been used to detect and classify student emotions in STEM online learning environments. It reviewed 57 peer-reviewed articles selected from an initial pool of 236, published between 2015 and 2023.

What It Covers
1. Text-Based Sentiment Analysis in Education (RQ1 \& RQ2)
The paper documents 37 studies on text-based emotion detection from sources like discussion forums, MOOC reviews, social media, and student feedback. Techniques covered include Naive Bayes, SVM, LSTM, CNN, BERT, and lexicon-based tools like VADER and SentiWordNet. Emotion classes used range from simple positive/negative/neutral to more nuanced categories like engaged, excited, bored, and frustrated.
Key challenges identified include emotion class overlapping, sarcasm and irony detection, unstructured data handling, fake/unreliable responses, and lack of labelled training data.
2. Facial Sentiment Analysis in Education (RQ3 \& RQ4)
The paper reviews 10 facial sentiment analysis (FSA) studies in educational settings. These include CNN-based systems, SVM classifiers, and hybrid models applied to detect emotions such as confusion, engagement, boredom, happiness, and neutral states. Notable included studies directly overlap with Maya's thesis topic — for example, Megahed \& Mohammed (2020) on adaptive e-learning using facial expressions and fuzzy logic, and a hybrid CNN study achieving 86–70\% accuracy on classroom engagement detection.
Key FSA challenges documented include limited accuracy in identifying subtle emotions like confusion and frustration, cultural variability in facial expressions, dependence on lighting and camera quality, occlusion effects (masks, glasses), and computational barriers to real-time analysis.
3. Other Modalities Acknowledged
The paper briefly acknowledges EEG and heart rate monitoring as complementary physiological modalities, but explicitly keeps its focus on text and facial data — which aligns cleanly with Maya's unimodal facial approach.
4. Future Directions
The paper calls for real-time FSA integration with adaptive systems, larger annotated education-specific datasets, multimodal fusion research, and Transformer-based architectures for improved classification — all of which frame gaps Maya's thesis can speak to.

