},
	annote = {I’m answering from the uploaded review paper only: Academic Emotion Classification Using FER: A Systematic Review.


What is the purpose of this review paper?


Short simple summary:The paper is a systematic literature review of student facial emotion recognition (FER) in online learning. It collects and compares prior studies on datasets, classifiers, preprocessing, feature extraction, results, strengths, and limitations. Evidence: “A systematic literature review (SLR) is conducted to extract and synthesize information” and “to obtain an overview of what has been done on the student FER system in the education field.”
What topic it reviews exactly:It reviews “student emotion recognition systems based on facial expressions” in “online learning.” Evidence: “This review article aims to investigate how machine learning and deep learning are used in student emotion recognition systems based on facial expressions in previous studies.”
Its scope:The review focuses on studies from the last five years selected through an SLR process, ending with 48 primary studies from 701 initial records. Its research questions cover approach type, datasets, classifiers, real-time capability, and accuracy/limitations on academic emotion datasets. Evidence: “701 publications were initially retrieved… 48 studies have been left for further review,” and the listed RQ1–RQ5.
What the review paper itself contributes:A structured synthesis of 48 studies, plus conclusions such as deep learning being most common, FER-2013 being the most used FER dataset, and DAiSEE being the most used academic emotion dataset.
What prior papers inside the review contribute:They contribute the actual datasets, models, and experimental results that the review summarizes; the review itself does not propose a new FER model. Evidence: Table 2 and the critical-review section summarize prior studies’ “emotion classifier,” “dataset,” “results,” “strength,” and “limitation.”


What kind of FER literature does it cover?




General FER: Yes. Many included studies “focus only on general emotions,” and the review has a full section on common FER datasets like FER-2013, JAFFE, CK+, KDEF, DISFA, and DISFA+.


Educational FER: Yes. The review explicitly targets “student FER system in the education field” and “student emotion during online learning.”


Academic emotions: Yes. It has a dedicated section “Academic Emotion Datasets,” including DAiSEE and OL-SFED.


Engagement detection: Yes. Several reviewed papers focus on engagement, including DAiSEE studies and engagement-level prediction.


Online learning affect: Yes. This is central to the paper; it repeatedly discusses “online learning” and student emotions in e-learning settings.


Classroom emotion recognition: Partially. The review includes some classroom-oriented or classroom-dataset studies, but the main scope is online learning. Evidence includes “classroom dataset” and “evaluate the classroom teaching effect.”


Multimodal affect recognition: Partially. The review mentions sensors such as “EEG, ECG, and EMG” and includes at least one paper with “video data + audio data + EEG signals data,” but the main focus remains FER.




Does the review discuss educational settings?


Yes.
The paper is explicitly framed around education and online learning: it says the SLR is about “the student FER system in the education field,” and it studies “classifying student emotion during online learning.” It also discusses educational problems such as lecturers being “unable to determine the students’ interest level in online learning” and the lack of engagement in MOOCs.


Does the review mention these datasets?




DAiSEE — Mentioned.Context: It is the main academic-emotion dataset in the review. The review says “DAiSEE is the most used academic emotion dataset” and describes it as containing “frustration, engagement, confusion, and boredom.”Relevance to your thesis: Directly relevant, because it is an educational facial-affect dataset in online learning.


FER-2013 — Mentioned.Context: It is the most used general FER dataset in the reviewed studies. The review says “FER-2013 is the most used dataset in the FER system.”Relevance to your thesis: Partially relevant, because it is important for facial-expression modeling, but it is not an academic-emotion dataset.


CK+ — Mentioned.Context: It is listed as a common FER benchmark and appears in many primary studies.Relevance to your thesis: Partially relevant, as a general FER benchmark rather than an educational dataset.


RAF-DB — Mentioned.Context: It appears in primary-study summaries such as Shen et al. and Gupta et al. Evidence: “JAFFE, CK+, and RAF-DB” and “FER-2013, CK+, RAF-DB.”Relevance to your thesis: Partially relevant, mainly as a general FER benchmark.


AffectNet — Mentioned.Context: It appears in the Savchenko et al. row as the dataset used.Relevance to your thesis: Partially relevant, as a general FER benchmark, not educational FER.


EmotiW — Not mentioned in the review.The closest nearby comparison I found is to “AFEW and VGAF datasets,” but the review text shown does not name EmotiW itself.Relevance to your thesis: Not clearly specified in the review.


Other educational datasets — Mentioned.Most clearly: OL-SFED, described as an online-learning spontaneous facial-expression dataset with “confusion, distraction, enjoyment, fatigue, and neutral.” The review also mentions smaller education-related/self-collected datasets such as “82 students that learn different online courses,” “classroom dataset,” and self-collected student-expression sets.Relevance to your thesis: Directly useful when the dataset is educational; partially useful when it is only small/self-collected.




What input modalities are covered in the review?




Face images: Yes. The review repeatedly discusses “facial images” and common FER image datasets.


Facial video / video sequences: Yes. CK+ is described as “593 video sequences,” DAiSEE as “9068 video clips,” and OL-SFED includes “1274 video clips.”


Landmarks / contours / feature points: Yes. The review discusses “landmarks or contours,” “68 facial landmarks,” and feature-point extraction methods such as ASM and Face++ landmarks.


Action units: Yes. DISFA/DISFA+ are presented with AU/FACS labels, and the evaluation section defines precision/recall for “a specific action unit (AU).”


Audio: Partially. One included paper uses “video data + audio data” and another “video data + audio data + EEG signals data,” but this is not the core review focus.


Text: Not reported in the review.


Physiological signals: Yes. The background mentions “EEG, ECG, and EMG,” and one included row uses EEG.


Multimodal combinations: Partially. Mentioned, but not the main scope; the review is centered on FER.




What model families are discussed?


Clearly discussed at review level:CNN, SVM, SVR, decision tree, random forest, KNN, DBN, LSTM, TRN, DFSTN, DCNN, hybrid CNN, hybrid DNN, BERN, SE-DAN, and MTCNN. Evidence: the taxonomy, Tables 3–4, and results summary.
Specific model names inside prior papers:ResNet appears as “SE-ResNet-50 (SENet)” and “ResNet-50”; VGG appears as “VGG16” and “VGG19”; Inception-V3 is also mentioned. These are reported as models used in primary studies, not as a main organizing family of the review.
Attention:Yes. It is explicitly mentioned in BERN as “bidirectional LSTM, and attention mechanism.”
Transfer learning / domain adaptation:Yes. The review mentions a study “based on CNNs and transfer learning” and says SE-DAN “can be used for transfer learning and domain adaptation.”
EfficientNet:Not reported in the review.
Vision Transformer:Not reported in the review.
GRU:Not reported in the review.
