Graph models:Not reported in the review.
Multimodal fusion:Not clearly specified in the review as a main model family. Multimodal combinations appear in some included studies, but the review is mainly FER-focused.


Does the review discuss these affective states relevant to my thesis?




BoredomExplicitly discussed: Yes.Educational context: Yes.Relevant to adaptive learning: Yes. The review says “Students who fail to understand and process information… may experience emotions like confusion, fatigue, boredom,” and also says “learning must adapt to the emotion of the students.”


ConfusionExplicitly discussed: Yes.Educational context: Yes.Relevant to adaptive learning: Yes. It is described as an academic emotion in DAiSEE and OL-SFED-related discussion, and linked to learning difficulty in online learning.


FrustrationExplicitly discussed: Yes.Educational context: Yes.Relevant to adaptive learning: Yes. It is one of DAiSEE’s affective states, and the review discusses low frustration recognition in DAiSEE-based work.


EngagementExplicitly discussed: Yes.Educational context: Yes.Relevant to adaptive learning: Yes. The review repeatedly treats engagement as central in online learning and notes that positive emotions can support learning interest and motivation.




Does the review discuss adaptation or only emotion recognition?


adaptation discussed
The review explicitly says “learning must adapt to the emotion of the students” and that “lecture materials or teaching style can be tailored.” However, its actual review questions and main synthesis are mostly about emotion recognition systems: approaches, datasets, classifiers, real-time capability, and accuracy/limitations. So adaptation is discussed as motivation, but not as the main technical review target.


What evaluation practices are discussed in the review?


Explicitly discussed:Accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Evidence: “FER is typically evaluated using metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score.”
Also discussed:Benchmark / state-of-the-art comparison, because the review notes comparisons with “state-of-the-art approaches and benchmarks” and “21 state-of-the-art methods.”
Real-time performance:Yes. One research question is “Do the existing student FER systems work in real time,” and the review reports that non-real-time systems are more common.
Robustness:Yes, but mainly inside summaries of prior papers’ strengths/limitations. Examples include “Robustness in classification” and comments about occlusion, head movement, illumination, and data imbalance.
Educational evaluation:Partially. The review mentions “pre- and postadoption assessment indicators” for OL-SFED and notes that in one engagement study the “direct correlation between engagement and actual task performance is unknown.”
Confusion matrix:Not reported in the review.
Cross-dataset evaluation:Not clearly specified in the review.
User studies:Not clearly specified in the review.
Simulation:Not reported in the review.
Offline evaluation:Not clearly specified in the review.


What gaps or limitations does the review identify?


Educational FER / online-learning FER:The review says there are “relatively few datasets… applicable for the purpose of FER in the context of online learning.” It also says it is “difficult to establish a connection between certain facial emotions and learning tasks.”
Face-only FER:The review warns that facial-expression-only recognition should be treated “with caution” because it may not capture “cultural and individual differences.”
DAiSEE-related gaps:The review highlights that many DAiSEE-based studies are not real-time, that one real-time system had only “60\%,” and that two DAiSEE studies did not fully classify student emotions because one used only negative emotions and another grouped emotions into engagement levels. It also notes low frustration recognition in one DAiSEE study.
Adaptive learning:Not clearly specified in the review as a reviewed technical gap. The paper motivates adaptation, but it does not systematically review adaptive-learning mechanisms.
Explainability:Partially. The review notes that DCNN models are “difficult to interpret,” but explainability is not a main review axis.
Lack of implementation:Not clearly specified in the review.
Weak evaluation:Yes, in several ways: class imbalance can make accuracy unreliable; engagement may not map directly to task performance; and many studies depend on limited datasets or naturalistic data that are hard to collect and evaluate.


